const hero = getPathwayHero('eb5-for-indian-h1b');

---

<PathwayLandingLayout
  title={title}
  metaDescription={metaDescription}
  canonicalUrl={canonicalUrl}
  h1={h1}
  subhead={subhead}
  heroImage={hero.image}
  heroImageWebp={hero.webp}
  heroImageAlt="EB-5 pathway for Indian H-1B professionals"
  relatedResearch={relatedResearch}
  secondaryCtaText="Talk to Someone About EB-5 for Indian H-1B Holders"
  breadcrumbTitle="EB-5 for Indian H-1B"
  keywords={[
    'EB-5 for Indian H-1B',
    'India EB-2 EB-3 backlog EB-5',
    'Indian tech workers EB-5',
    'H-1B consulting EB-5',
  ]}
>
  <h2>India chargeability and why EB-5 enters the conversation</h2>
  <p>
    Many Indian H-1B professionals eventually reach an employment-based green card petition (typically EB-2 or EB-3)
    only to find that India-chargeable demand has historically produced long queues under per-country limits. Exact wait
    times change and should be checked against the current
    <a
      href="https://travel.state.gov/content/travel/en/legal/visa-law0/visa-bulletin.html"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener noreferrer">Visa Bulletin</a
    >; this page does not cite specific multi-year estimates that go stale. EB-5 uses a different visa allocation
    structure, including reserved categories that some Indian professionals evaluate as a parallel track. EB-5 still has
    its own rules, capital requirements, and category dynamics. It is not a guaranteed faster outcome. For shared H-1B
    concurrent-filing and employer-independence mechanics (without repeating them here), see
    <a href="/pathways/h1b-to-green-card">H-1B to green card via EB-5</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>A separate process from your H-1B employer petition</h2>
  <p>
    Once an EB-5 petition is filed, it does not depend on H-1B lottery selection, PERM sponsorship, or continued
    employer willingness to pursue an employment-based case. That separation is why some Indian professionals treat EB-5
    as a backup or primary track while remaining on H-1B. Deeper framing for Indian tech workers is in
    <a href="/research/best-immigration-backup-plan-indian-tech-workers-2026"
      >Best Immigration Backup Plan for Indian Tech Workers</a
    >.
  </p>

  <h2>IT staffing and consulting H-1B holders</h2>
  <p>
    A large segment of Indian H-1B professionals work through IT staffing or consulting arrangements rather than as
    direct hires of the end client. Those arrangements can add layers of uncertainty: project placement, vendor
    contracts, and sponsorship that sits with the consulting employer rather than the worksite company. EB-5's
    employer-independence applies the same way regardless of consulting versus direct-hire status: the green card case
    stands on the investor's qualifying investment and petition, not on who signs the H-1B or who staffs the project.
    Individual eligibility and timing still need attorney review.
  </p>

  <h2>Next steps</h2>
  <p>
    For investment thresholds, I-526E filing sequence, and project selection basics aimed at Indian nationals, see
    <a href="/pathways/eb5-for-indian-investors">EB-5 for Indian investors</a>. For the full process overview, see the
    <a href="/eb5-investment-process">EB-5 investment process</a>.
  </p>
</PathwayLandingLayout>
